      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of ways to distribute six identical copies of a book such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Total number of ways to distribute the books without any restrictions:**\n   Each of the six books can be either in the library or checked out. Therefore, there are \\(2^6 = 64\\) possible distributions of the books if there were no restrictions.\n\n2. **Subtract the cases where all books are in the library or all books are checked out:**\n   - There is exactly 1 way where all 6 books are in the library.\n   - There is exactly 1 way where all 6 books are checked out.\n   So, we subtract these 2 cases from the total number of distributions.\n   Thus, the number of valid distributions is \\(64 - 2 = 62\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to distribute the books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).",
