    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to distribute six identical copies of a book such that at least one book is in the library and at least one book is checked out. Let's denote the number of books in the library by \\( x \\) and the number of books checked out by \\( y \\). Since there are six books in total, we have the equation:\n\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\n\nWe also need to satisfy the conditions \\( x \\geq 1 \\) and \\( y \\geq 1 \\). We can rewrite these conditions as \\( x - 1 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( y - 1 \\geq 0 \\). This transforms our equation into:\n\n\\[ (x - 1) + (y - 1) = 5 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the number of non-negative integer solutions to this equation. This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable items into 2 indistinguishable parts (the remaining books after assigning one book to the library and one book to be checked out). The number of solutions is given by the \"stars and bars\" theorem, which states that the number of ways to distribute \\( n \\) indistinguishable items into \\( k \\) indistinguishable parts is \\( \\binom{n+k-1}{k-1} \\). Here, \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\), so we have:\n\n\\[ \\binom{5+2-1}{2-1} = \\binom{6}{1} = 6 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to distribute six identical books such that at least one is in the library and at least one is checked out is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
