      "text": "To find the number of different ways to arrange six identical books where at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out, we can use the principle of complementary counting. First, we'll find the total number of ways to distribute the books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out, and then subtract the cases where no books are in the library (i.e., all books are checked out) and no books are checked out (i.e., all books are in the library).\n\n### Total Ways to Distribute Books\n\nThe total number of ways to distribute the books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out is equivalent to the total number of non-empty subsets of a set of 6 books. This is because each subset represents a valid distribution of the books.\n\nThe total number of subsets of a set of 6 books is \\(2^6 = 64\\). Since we want non-empty subsets, we subtract 1 (the empty subset) from this total:\n\\[\n64 - 1 = 63\n\\]\n\n### Ways with No Books in the Library\n\nThere is only one way to distribute the books such that no books are in the library, which is to have all 6 books checked out. This is:\n\\[\n1\n\\]\n\n### Ways with No Books Checked Out\n\nThere is only one way to distribute the books such that no books are checked out, which is to have all 6 books in the library. This is:\n\\[\n1\n\\]\n\n### Valid Ways\n\nThe number of valid ways to distribute the books such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out is the total number of ways minus the cases where no books are in the library minus the cases where no books are checked out:\n\\[\n63 - 1 - 1 = 61\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways to distribute the books is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{61}\n\\]",
